# Scale limits: lims

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

mtcars = Giggly.Datasets.mtcars()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`lims()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/lims.html), which covers `xlim()` and `ylim()`
too, in the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's.
An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the
page is also a list of what remains.

`xlim()` and `ylim()` set the limits of the x and y scales, and `lims()` those of any scale, named
by its aesthetic, without writing out the scale itself. Values outside the limits become missing and
are dropped from the plot; giving the larger limit first reverses the scale, and `NA` leaves a limit
to the data. To zoom without dropping data, ggplot2 sets the limits on the coordinate system
instead, with `coord_cartesian()`. Giggly has `Giggly.Plot.xlim/3`, `Giggly.Plot.ylim/3` and
`Giggly.Plot.lims/2`, `nil` leaving a limit to the data, but no `coord_cartesian()`. A plot is
described with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with `Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG document. The
setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells write `Plot.new/2`, and defines `render`, which draws
a plot and shows it below the cell with `Kino.Image`. The data in most examples is
`Giggly.Datasets.mtcars/0`, R's `mtcars`: 32 cars from the 1974 *Motor Trend* US magazine, which the
setup cell binds to `mtcars`, R's name for it.

## Zooming into a range

```r
# Zoom into a specified area
ggplot(mtcars, aes(mpg, wt)) +
  geom_point() +
  xlim(15, 20)
```

ggplot2 drops the cars outside 15 to 20 mpg, and warns that it removed them. Giggly drops them
without a warning, since drawing a plot has no side effects.

```elixir
mtcars
|> Plot.new(%{x: :mpg, y: :wt})
|> Plot.geom_point()
|> Plot.xlim(15, 20)
|> render.()
```

## A reversed scale

```r
# reverse scale
ggplot(mtcars, aes(mpg, wt)) +
  geom_point() +
  xlim(20, 15)
```

With the larger limit first the axis runs from 20 down to 15.

```elixir
mtcars
|> Plot.new(%{x: :mpg, y: :wt})
|> Plot.geom_point()
|> Plot.xlim(20, 15)
|> render.()
```

## One limit from the data

```r
# with automatic lower limit
ggplot(mtcars, aes(mpg, wt)) +
  geom_point() +
  xlim(NA, 20)
```

R's `NA` leaves the lower limit to the data, the smallest `mpg`, 10.4; Giggly writes it `nil`.

```elixir
mtcars
|> Plot.new(%{x: :mpg, y: :wt})
|> Plot.geom_point()
|> Plot.xlim(nil, 20)
|> render.()
```

## Limits wider than the data

Both plots give the colour scale all three cylinder counts, so a count has the same colour in
each, although each plot's data has only some of them.

```r
# You can also supply limits that are larger than the data.
# This is useful if you want to match scales across different plots
small <- subset(mtcars, cyl == 4)
big <- subset(mtcars, cyl > 4)

ggplot(small, aes(mpg, wt, colour = factor(cyl))) +
  geom_point() +
  lims(colour = c("4", "6", "8"))

ggplot(big, aes(mpg, wt, colour = factor(cyl))) +
  geom_point() +
  lims(colour = c("4", "6", "8"))
```

The cell keeps the rows R's `subset()` keeps, each column's values where `cyl` passes the test.
Giggly writes `factor(cyl)` as `{:factor, [:cyl]}`, and names each limit as the column holds it,
the numbers `4`, `6` and `8`, where R names them as text. The legend of each plot has a key for
every limit, but a point drawn only in the keys its own data holds.

```elixir
subset = fn keep? ->
  kept = Enum.map(Keyword.fetch!(mtcars, :cyl), keep?)

  Enum.map(mtcars, fn {name, values} ->
    rows =
      values
      |> Enum.zip(kept)
      |> Enum.filter(fn {_value, keep} -> keep end)
      |> Enum.map(fn {value, _keep} -> value end)

    {name, rows}
  end)
end

small = subset.(fn cyl -> cyl == 4 end)
big = subset.(fn cyl -> cyl > 4 end)

small
|> Plot.new(%{x: :mpg, y: :wt, colour: {:factor, [:cyl]}})
|> Plot.geom_point()
|> Plot.lims(colour: [4, 6, 8])
|> render.()
```

```elixir
big
|> Plot.new(%{x: :mpg, y: :wt, colour: {:factor, [:cyl]}})
|> Plot.geom_point()
|> Plot.lims(colour: [4, 6, 8])
|> render.()
```

## Two ways to limit an axis

`Sys.Date()` is today's date, so the data is the last 60 days, each with a random price, drawn as
a line with a smoothed curve through it.

```r
# There are two ways of setting the axis limits: with limits or
# with coordinate systems. They work in two rather different ways.

set.seed(1)
last_month <- Sys.Date() - 0:59
df <- data.frame(
  date = last_month,
  price = c(rnorm(30, mean = 15), runif(30) + 0.2 * (1:30))
)

p <- ggplot(df, aes(date, price)) +
  geom_line() +
  stat_smooth()

p
```

Not in Giggly yet: `stat_smooth()`.

## Limits that drop data

```r
# Setting the limits with the scale discards all data outside the range.
p + lims(x= c(Sys.Date() - 30, NA), y = c(10, 20))
```

The smooth is fitted only to the rows left inside the limits.

Not in Giggly yet: `stat_smooth()`.

## Limits that zoom

```r
# For changing x or y axis limits **without** dropping data
# observations use [coord_cartesian()]. Setting the limits on the
# coordinate system performs a visual zoom.
p + coord_cartesian(xlim =c(Sys.Date() - 30, NA), ylim = c(10, 20))
```

Not in Giggly yet: `coord_cartesian()` and its `xlim` and `ylim` options, and `stat_smooth()`.
Giggly's plots are in Cartesian coordinates, as ggplot2's are by default, but nothing sets their
options.
